<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Recuay</h1><p class="st-meta">1 CE – 700 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A highland culture of Peru's Callejón de Huaylas known for fine kaolin pottery, stone sculpture of warriors and elite residences built around fortified compounds.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Recuay culture arose around the start of the Common Era in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callejon-de-huaylas">Callejón de Huaylas</a> and the upper valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ancash-region">Ancash</a>, in the same region where <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chavin">Chavín</a> had flourished. Communities built hilltop settlements and stone compounds above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-river-peru">Santa</a> river valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Recuay society was led by local chiefs and warrior elites who lived in walled compounds and were buried in stone tombs with offerings. Herding of llamas and alpacas and high-altitude farming supported the economy. The culture is known for white kaolin pottery painted in red and black, often modelled as figures of chiefs, women and llamas, and for stone sculptures and lintels showing warriors with shields and trophy heads. Its contacts with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moche">Moche</a> on the coast were close.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Recuay declined around 600 to 700 CE, possibly under pressure from the expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wari-empire">Wari Empire</a>, whose influence reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ancash-region">Ancash</a>. Its stone sculpture tradition remained a reference point for later highland cultures.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/recuay.jpg" alt="Recuay" width="258" height="367"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_cultura_recuay.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1 CE – 700 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancestor worship</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Recuay culture, Huaylas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=350&amp;focus=recuay" aria-label="Recuay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2673 2648 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Recuay lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2868 2783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2993 2904h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2993 2904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Peru. <a href="/atlas?year=350&amp;focus=recuay">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

